<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gedaliah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gedaliah son of Ahikam was a Judahite official whom the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> appointed governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-jerusalem-587-bce">destruction of Jerusalem in 587</a> or 586 BCE. He governed from Mizpah and urged the survivors to serve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, but within months he was assassinated by Ishmael son of Nethaniah, a member of the royal family. Fearing Babylonian reprisals, many Judahites then fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; the Fast of Gedaliah still marks his death.</p></div>
